## 2. Risks of loading secrets from a **.env** file  

| Issue | Why it matters | Typical exploitation scenario |
|-------|----------------|------------------------------|
| **Plain‑text storage** | Secrets are stored in clear text on disk; anyone with file‑system access (including backup snapshots, CI logs, or a compromised container) can read them. | An attacker who gains read access to the container can copy the `.env` file and obtain DB passwords, API keys, etc. |
| **Accidental commit** | `.env` files are often unintentionally added to source control, leaking credentials publicly. | A developer pushes the repo to GitHub, exposing keys on the internet. |
| **Lack of rotation** | Updating a secret requires updating a file on every host, which is error‑prone. | Old credentials remain in some machines after a rotation, creating an “in‑flight” window for abuse. |
| **No audit / access control** | File permissions are the only protection; there is no central audit of who accessed or changed a secret. | Insider threats can copy the file without any trace. |

