## 🎯 Why Using a Secrets Manager Is Better (Even If You Add `.env` Later)

| Problem with a plain `.env` file | How a proper secrets manager solves it |
|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------|
| **Accidental commit** – `.env` can be added to source control and leak credentials. | Centralised secret store (AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault, Azure Key Vault, GCP Secret Manager) keeps values out of the repo; you only store a reference or a short‑lived token. |
| **Rotation difficulty** – Changing a secret means editing many files and redeploying. | API‑driven rotation: you can update a secret in the manager and the next pod/container picks up the new value automatically. |
| **No audit trail** – Git shows the secret value, and anyone with repo read‑access can see it. | Secrets managers keep an audit log of read/write access, supporting compliance (PCI‑DSS, HIPAA, etc.). |
| **Environment leakage** – Multiple environments (dev, staging, prod) often share the same `.env` file. | Each environment gets its own secret version/namespace; policies enforce least‑privilege access. |
| **Plain‑text storage on disk** – If the container is compromised, the file is readable. | Secrets can be delivered via memory‑only mechanisms (e.g., `AWS SDK` with IAM roles, `Vault Agent` with file‑system “template” that can be zero‑ed). |

